#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "669a636b-6611-50b8-84df-2a22afa2af9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi^2-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669a636b-6611-50b8-84df-2a22afa2af9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi^2-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302504} :answer-field/id #uuid "1747833e-00a1-5a08-8b51-d14818791771" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a32abdd-07d2-5cd6-a901-13295b56bc62" :question/math-academy-id "q-352011" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+2\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =((4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+8(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+2+0-8)-0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-6 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
